# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository.
#
# This is not housekeeping. The project is developed on Windows and deployed to
# Linux, and a CRLF file breaks two things badly:
#
#   * every `run:` block in a GitHub Actions workflow — bash reads the trailing
#     \r as part of the command and fails with an error that names a token
#     nobody wrote
#   * shell scripts and entrypoints inside the container, the same way
#
# Rule files are covered too. Their signatures happen to survive a line-ending
# change, because PyYAML normalises line breaks when it parses — but that is a
# property we should not have to rely on, and there is a test asserting it.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the files where it matters most.
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.toml   text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.ts     text eol=lf
*.tsx    text eol=lf
*.css    text eol=lf
*.html   text eol=lf
*.svg    text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf

# Never touched.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.ico    binary
*.parquet binary
*.duckdb binary
*.pdf    binary
